 \begin{abstract}
-We study how a finite junction transfers energy from a prepared longitudinal line wave into a three-dimensional elastic medium. A scalar axial channel is coupled to a vector harmonic lattice by passive paired central-force springs, with no incident bulk wave. The paired geometry produces axial and radial source patterns whose forces and reciprocal response follow from one Hamiltonian. At the dimensionless reference frequency $\omega=1.2$, approximately $12.6\%$ of the incident power enters the separated lower acoustic cluster, identified as quasi-transverse (qT); this is about $91.9\%$ of the radiated bulk power. An exact centered source-sector reduction retains the infinite exterior through a projected Green matrix. Separately implemented frequency-domain formulations corroborate the reference coefficient. Direct packet evolution from zero bulk displacement and velocity supplies further numerical corroboration when compared with the frequency-domain response using the packet's energy spectrum. Source controls and a decomposition of the full state show that axial and radial patterns affect the modal power through their combined response and interference. A perturbation bound quantifies joint-coupling sensitivity of the computed response. The result connects local source geometry to incident-normalized elastic radiation in a specified finite reciprocal junction.
